# ShramSetu — there is deliberately NO root .env.
#
# ShramSetu is a polyglot microservice stack and each service reads its own
# environment file from its own directory:
#
#   server/.env      <- Node API        (port 8000)
#                      copy from server/.env.example
#   ai-service/.env  <- Python AI svc   (port 8100)
#                      copy from ai-service/.env.example
#   frontend/        <- Vite dev server; reads only VITE_-prefixed vars,
#                      so it normally needs no env file at all.
#
# docker-compose.yml requires NO environment file: it starts MongoDB only and
# publishes 27017. To use it, point the server at it in server/.env:
#
#   MONGODB_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/shramsetu
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Never commit a real .env or a real API key. The per-service
#    .env.example files are the templates and contain placeholders only.
# 2. Two values are secrets and have no safe default:
#      JWT_SECRET_KEY  (server)   — long random string
#      AI_INTERNAL_KEY (both)     — must MATCH on both sides; the Node server
#                                   sends it as X-Internal-Key on every call
#    Generate one with:
#      node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# 3. API keys (GROQ_API_KEY, SARVAM_API_KEY, GOOGLE_API_KEY,
#    GOOGLE_TRANSLATE_API_KEY) are optional: without them the chatbot and the
#    voice features fall back or degrade honestly. See each .env.example.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Note on history
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file used to document a Postgres + Python-FastAPI-backend stack
# (POSTGRES_PASSWORD, FAST2SMS_API_KEY, SMTP_*, S3_*, REDIS_URL and a
# backend/app/main.py production check). That architecture was replaced by the
# Node API + MongoDB service. None of those variables were read by the current
# code, so they were removed rather than left here to mislead — a stale config
# template is worse than no template.
